\section{Introduction}

Current paradigms of human-AI integration focus on transferring cognitive processes into artificial systems. This introduces challenges of continuity, control, and dependency on external infrastructure.

An alternative direction is integration of computational processes into biological substrate. Rather than migrating cognition, this approach augments existing biological systems through embedded computational interfaces.

This paper does not claim that such a system has been realized. Instead, it demonstrates that:
\begin{itemize}
\item Individual enabling technologies exist
\item Their functional roles can be clearly defined
\item A staged pathway toward integration can be constructed
\end{itemize}

\section{Component Foundations}

\subsection{Gene Delivery Systems}

Lentiviral vectors are widely used for stable genetic delivery into both dividing and non-dividing cells \cite{naldini_1996, clinical_lentiviral_2018}. Their established use in clinical gene therapy demonstrates reliable integration into host genomes.

\subsection{Optogenetic Control}

Light-controlled gene expression systems enable precise temporal regulation of cellular processes \cite{opto_t7rnap_2017}. These systems provide a controllable interface between external signals and biological activity.

\subsection{Engineered Microbial Systems}

Engineered \textit{E. coli} have been demonstrated to produce and secrete functional biomolecules in vivo \cite{prot3ect_2023}. This establishes the feasibility of persistent biological “factories” within host environments.

\subsection{RNA-Based Regulation}

RNA molecules function as programmable regulators of gene expression and cellular behavior \cite{rna_programming_2016}. Synthetic biology has demonstrated the construction of RNA-based logic and control systems within cells.

\section{Proposed Architecture}

The Braided Mind Architecture is defined as a closed-loop system consisting of:

\begin{enumerate}
\item A computational controller
\item A signal encoding interface (optical)
\item Engineered biological mediators (microbial or cellular)
\item Target cellular systems
\item Feedback measurement channels
\end{enumerate}

Signal flow is defined as:

\[
\text{Controller} \rightarrow \text{Signal Encoding} \rightarrow \text{Biological Interface} \rightarrow \text{Cellular Response} \rightarrow \text{Feedback} \rightarrow \text{Controller}
\]

Each element of this loop has independent experimental precedent. The novelty lies in their integration.

\section{Implementation Pathway}

\subsection{Stage 1: In Vitro Loop Construction}

Construct a closed-loop system in controlled conditions:
\begin{itemize}
\item Optogenetic bacterial control of RNA output
\item Mammalian cell response measurement
\item Software-driven feedback adjustment
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Stage 2: Tissue-Level Systems}

Extend the system into structured biological environments:
\begin{itemize}
\item Organoid or tissue model integration
\item Signal propagation measurement
\item Stability testing
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Stage 3: Localized In Vivo Demonstration}

Demonstrate function in living systems:
\begin{itemize}
\item Localized biological interface
\item Controlled signaling
\item Measurable biological modulation
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Stage 4: Integrated Systems Research}

Investigate scaling challenges:
\begin{itemize}
\item Multi-channel coordination
\item Long-term stability
\item Safety and failure modes
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion}

The architecture described here is not dependent on a single breakthrough. Instead, it represents a synthesis of existing capabilities into a unified system.

The primary challenge is integration:
\begin{itemize}
\item Signal fidelity across biological media
\item Control precision
\item Long-term stability
\end{itemize}

Addressing these challenges requires systematic experimentation rather than speculative design.

\section{Conclusion}

We have outlined a biologically integrated AI architecture grounded in existing scientific capabilities. While the complete system has not been realized, its components are supported by peer-reviewed work, and a staged pathway toward implementation can be defined.

The next step is experimental validation of closed-loop biological communication systems.

